A Test of Couplets and of Prose.

Romeo, a young man with a remarkable patience.
Juliet, a likewise young woman of remarkable grace.
Peter, a servant to the Capulets, who cannot count.

Act I: The trial.

Scene I: A scene that ends in a couplet, as it should.

[Enter Romeo and Juliet]

Juliet:
 Thou art the sum of a big big big cat
 and a cat. Speak thy mind! Thou art a hat.

[Exit Juliet]
[Enter Peter]

Scene II: A servant speaks prose, and the scene ends badly.

Peter:
 Thou art the sum of a cat and a big big big big big big cat. Speak thy mind!

Romeo:
 Thou art the sum of thyself and a cat.
 Speak thy mind! Thou art a big big big dog.

[Exeunt]
